              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.191,
            0.086,
            0.807,
            0.266
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_18_equation_1.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix}\\overline{7} & \\overline{8} & \\overline{9} & \\overline{9} \\\\\\overline{4\\left ( 4q + 2 \\right ) = 50} & \\overline{5} & \\overline{8} & \\overline{9} \\\\\\overline{4\\left ( 4q \\right ) = 50} - 2 & \\overline{8} & \\overline{9} & \\overline{9} \\\\\\overline{16q = 48} & \\overline{9} & \\overline{9} & \\overline{9} \\\\\\overline{q = 3} & \\overline{9} & \\overline{9} & \\overline{9} \\\\\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{array}{l}\\overline{8} \\\\\\overline{8} \\\\\\overline{- 2\\left ( 3 - 4x \\right ) = 14} \\\\\\overline{- 6v - 8x = 14} \\\\\\overline{- 8x = 14 + 6} \\\\\\overline{- 8x = 20} \\\\\\overline{x = - \\frac{20}{8}} \\\\\\end{array}\\quad\\begin{array}{l}\\overline{9} \\\\\\overline{9} \\\\\\overline{3\\left ( 7y + 4 \\right ) = 9 + 5y} \\\\\\overline{7y + 4 = \\frac{9}{3} + 5y} \\\\\\overline{7y + 4 = 3 + 5y} \\\\\\overline{7y - 5y + 4 = 3} \\\\\\overline{2y = 4 - 3} \\\\\\underline{y = \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\\end{array}\\end{pmatrix}"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.293,
            0.453,
            0.317
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7.4 A Pinch of History"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.327,
            0.849,
            0.463
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Forming expressions using symbols and solving equations with such expressions was an important component of mathematical explorations in ancient India. This area of mathematics was termed bījagaṇita, also now known as algebra. The word bīja means seed. Just as a tree is hidden inside a seed, the answer to a problem is hidden inside an unknown number. Solving the problem is like helping the tree grow — step by step, we discover what is hidden."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.463,
            0.849,
            0.616
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We have seen "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Brahmagupta’s",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " contributions to different areas of mathematics like integers and fractions. In Chapter 18 of his book Brāhmasphuṭasiddhānta (628 CE), he also explained how to add, subtract, and multiply unknown numbers using letters — similar to how we use x or y today. This chapter was one of the earliest known works in algebra in history. As renowned mathematician and Fields Medalist David Mumford remarked, ‘Brahmagupta is the key person in the creation of algebra as we know it’."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.616,
            0.848,
            0.712
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the 8th century, Indian mathematical ideas were translated into Arabic. They influenced a well-known mathematician named "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Al-Khwarizmi",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", who lived in present-day Iraq. Around 825 CE, he wrote a book called Hisab al-jabr wal-muqabala, which means ‘calculation by restoring and balancing’."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.712,
            0.848,
            0.789
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "These ideas spread even further. By the 12th century, Al-Khwarizmi’s book was translated into Latin and brought to "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Europe",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", where it became very popular. The word al-jabr from his book gave us the word "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "algebra",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", which we also still use today."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.789,
            0.849,
            0.887
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Similar to how we use letters from the alphabet today to represent unknowns, ancient Indian mathematicians from the time of Brahmagupta used distinct symbols like "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "yā, kā, nī, pī, lo",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", etc., for different unknowns. The symbol yā was short for yāvat-tāvat (meaning ‘as much as needed’). Others like kā and nī referred to as the first letters"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 8,
